<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aurora de Chile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aurora de Chile was the first newspaper published in Chile, issued weekly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> from February 1812 to April 1813 on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press brought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Edited by the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camilo-henriquez">Camilo Henríquez</a> under the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a>, it spread the ideas of independence and popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. It was succeeded by El Monitor Araucano.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurora-de-chile.jpg" alt="Aurora de Chile" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auroradechile-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Until the Aurora appeared, the only printed newspapers in Chile came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and arrived after long delays. The political philosophy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and the documents of the revolutions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> were known and had been translated, but apart from a possible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> press for religious material in earlier decades there was no local press to reprint them or to print Chilean revolutionary writing.</p><p>After the first Government Junta took power on 18 September 1810, attempts to obtain a press from the Spanish government and then from the revolutionary junta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> failed, the second after the death of Mariano Moreno. On 24 November 1811 the Swedish-American Mathew Hoevel, known in Spanish as Mateo Arnaldo Hoevel, brought the Galloway into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, American printers, among them Samuel Burr Johnston, and arms and munitions for the independence movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The paper ran to four printed pages of two columns each and appeared every Thursday, from 13 February 1812. Its editor, Camilo Henríquez González, was the first to argue seriously for Chilean independence, and the Aurora was the first widely read publication to introduce Chilean readers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>. It was dominated by politics and political philosophy.</p><p>In articles by Manuel de Salas, Juan Egaña, Manuel José Gandarillas and the Guatemalan Antonio José de Irisarri, the paper set out the principles of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, self-government, the election of officials and the separation of powers. Henríquez added sharp, timely commentary on the doings of the monarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>American influence weighed heavily with Chile's liberal revolutionary intellectuals. The Aurora reprinted speeches by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> and other American revolutionaries, who became heroes in the Chilean press. On 1 April 1813 the paper was turned into El Monitor Araucano, after just over a year of weekly issues.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aurora de Chile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>José Miguel Carrera seized power in a series of coups and introduced a provisional constitution, the first national flag and the newspaper <b>Aurora de Chile</b>, edited by Camilo Henríquez.</blockquote><a href="/state/patria-vieja">Patria Vieja</a></li></ul></section>
